\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A neutral nearest-neighbor observation survives}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{Apply \ref{fdip-00J5} to the following large-mass family. Choose any dimension #{d\geq1}, a declared graded Clifford module, and a direction #{j} with #{N_j\geq3}; the other periods may be any positive integers. Put #{x=y+e_j} and take the one-edge path #{\gamma:y\to x} and #{M=\gamma_j}. This example uses distinct forward and reverse neighbors in direction #{j}. Set
##{\mu=m+2dc_W/a^2,\qquad K_U=\mu I+V_U,\qquad R=d/a+2dc_W/a^2.}}

\p{Then #{\|V_U\|\leq R} uniformly over all link configurations. If #{\mu>R}, the Neumann expansion gives
##{K_U^{-1}=\mu^{-1}I-\mu^{-2}V_U+E_U,\qquad
\|E_U\|\leq\frac{R^2}{\mu^3(1-R/\mu)}.}}

\p{There is exactly one positive oriented edge from #{y} to #{x} in direction #{j}, and #{N_j\geq3} keeps the forward and reverse neighbors distinct. In a declared orthonormal basis of the site and spin fibers, its matrix block is
##{(V_U)_{yx}=\left(\frac{\gamma_j}{2a}-\frac{c_W}{a^2}I\right)h_\gamma^{-1}.}}

\p{Since #{\trace\gamma_j=\trace(\Gamma\gamma_j\Gamma)=-\trace\gamma_j} and #{\gamma_j^2=I}, the exact dressed expectation satisfies
##{\langle B_{\gamma,\gamma_j}\rangle_U=\frac{n_s}{2a\mu^2}+\varepsilon_U,\qquad
|\varepsilon_U|\leq\frac{n_sR^2}{\mu^3(1-R/\mu)}.}}

\p{Here #{\|\gamma_j\|=|h_\gamma|=1} and #{|\trace A|\leq n_s\|A\|} bound the error. Therefore the same bound survives every positive normalized connection average in this class. For example, if
##{\mu-R>4aR^2,}}

\p{then #{\operatorname{Re}\langle B_{\gamma,\gamma_j}\rangle>n_s/(4a\mu^2)>0}. This is a uniform large-mass family on every finite periodic lattice having such a direction, with any nonzero integer charge and any chosen gauge action. It proves that neutral dressed correlations need not vanish, whereas the bare charged off-diagonal kernel vanishes at every positive mass. It does not assert nonvanishing for every path, spin insertion or mass.}
